WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.800
Let’s test Rule thirteen against the pecking order.

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:13.975
You’ll meet that properly in lesson seven, but the short version is that power normally gives way to sail, and sail gives way to vessels engaged in fishing.

3
00:00:14.225 --> 00:00:17.150
So, a powerboat overtaking a sailboat.

4
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.075
Who keeps clear?

5
00:00:19.325 --> 00:00:20.925
The powerboat.

6
00:00:21.175 --> 00:00:23.950
Both rules agree, so that one’s easy.

7
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.800
Now flip it.

8
00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:31.400
A sailboat, flying along in a good breeze, comes up astern of a slow powerboat.

9
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.325
Who keeps clear?

10
00:00:34.325 --> 00:00:35.925
The sailboat.

11
00:00:36.175 --> 00:00:40.600
She’s overtaking, and overtaking overrides the pecking order.

12
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:44.700
It’s the same with a fishing vessel overtaking anyone.

13
00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:46.675
She keeps clear.

14
00:00:46.925 --> 00:00:49.150
So here’s your exam habit.

15
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:56.750
Whenever a question describes one vessel coming up on another from astern, stop looking at what kind of vessels they are.

16
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.525
The overtaking vessel keeps clear.

17
00:00:59.775 --> 00:01:01.450
Every time.

18
00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.350
That’s lesson five.

19
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.300
The steering rules apply only in sight.

20
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:11.475
Between sailboats, port tack and the windward boat keep clear.

21
00:01:11.725 --> 00:01:16.000
And anyone overtaking keeps clear until she’s finally past.

22
00:01:16.250 --> 00:01:22.825
Next time, we’ll cover the two situations you’ll meet most often in a powerboat: head-on and crossing.
